From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 21:05:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: samsung: add the gs201 CSIS receiver and CSIS WDMA
 driver

The gs201 (Tensor G2) camera block receives MIPI CSI-2 on eight CSIS
links, a newer version (0x05040000) of the receiver in imx-mipi-csis,
and its DMA block's CSIS WDMA contexts write a link's frames straight
to memory. Stock uses that path for its RAW output, without the rest of
the ISP, which is all a software ISP needs.

Add a driver for one link and one WDMA context as a media device:
sensor -> CSIS subdev -> video node, raw Bayer 8 and 10 bit packed as
on the bus (the WDMA has no packed 12-bit format), D-PHY or C-PHY from
the endpoint's bus type. Several links may share a WDMA context (the
four cameras of the Pixel 7 Pro all use context 0) and the routing
registers are shared, so one instance streams at a time.

At stream on the driver votes the ACPM CAM and INT DVFS domains (a
C-PHY link needs a higher CAM rate: the main camera's three trios
overflowed the link's image FIFO at the D-PHY vote), powers and
configures the DC-PHY, releases the link reset in the CSIS sysreg,
routes the link to its WDMA context with the EBUF bypassed, and
programs the link (channel configuration with the pixel mode and
virtual channel, the separate shadow update, the PHY common control)
and the WDMA (control, format code, width-only resolution, a 64-byte
aligned stride, the slot mask and the common DMA setup).

Buffers come from vb2-dma-contig in a reserved pool below 4 GiB, since
the WDMA takes 32-bit addresses and its System MMU stays in bypass.
They are allocated cached by default and synced at dequeue: libcamera's
software ISP reads every frame with the CPU, and an uncached mapping
cost tens of milliseconds a frame. A buffer whose frame end never comes
is completed with an error, so stop_streaming finds none left behind;
buffers queued before start_streaming are taken only once it runs.
Frame-sync events on the subdev pace the sensor's controls.

The register offsets and interrupt bits are from Google's GPL gs201
device tree and lwis headers; the link and WDMA programming follows what
the stock camera software writes. gs201-csis-regs.h marks what is known
and what is inferred. Error, frame and line counters and the link,
WDMA and sysreg registers are in debugfs, and a number of module
parameters (all off or at the working value by default) override single
programming choices for bring-up.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
